    <DataNode elementId="SULFOQUINOVOSYLDIACYLGLYCEROL" textLabel="a 1,2-diacyl-3-(6-sulfo-α-D-quinovosyl)-sn-glycerol" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="25244599" dataSource="PubChem-compound" />
      <Graphics centerX="200" centerY="400" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment>|FRAME: SULFOQUINOVOSYLDIACYLGLYCEROL &quot;Sulfoquinovosyl diacylglycerol&quot;| (SQDG) is an abundant class of sulfolipids composed of a glycoside of |FRAME: CPD-10247| and |FRAME: 1-2-Diglycerides diacylglycerol|, which are found in many photosynthetic organisms, including all photosynthetic plants, algae, cyanobacteria, and purple sulfur and non-sulfur bacteria. SQDG is synthesized from |FRAME: UDP-SULFOQUINOVOSE| by the action of |FRAME: EC-2.4.1.M2|. The synthesis of SQDG is not crucial under normal growth conditions. It only becomes important when phosphate is limited |CITS: [9465123]|. Under such conditions, the increase of SQDG levels compensates for the decrease in phospholipids and ensures a constant proportion of anionic lipids in photosynthetic membranes |CITS:[14675442]|. SQDG is localized in photosynthetic membranes (in plants in the thylakoid membranes), where it is closely associated with certain membrane proteins.</Comment>
